#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:22
msgid "1. Making your first figure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:24
msgid ""
"This tutorial covers the basics of creating a figure using PyGMT - a Python "
"interface for the Generic Mapping Tools (GMT). It will only use "
"the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.coast` method for plotting. Later tutorials will "
"address other PyGMT methods."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:31
msgid "Loading the library"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:33
msgid ""
"The first step is to import :mod:`pygmt`. All methods and figure generation "
"are accessible from the :mod:`pygmt` top level package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:38
msgid ""
"import pygmt\n"
"from pygmt.params import Frame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:48
msgid "Creating a figure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:50
msgid ""
"All figure generation in PyGMT is handled by the :class:`pygmt.Figure` "
"class. Start a new figure by creating an instance of this class:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:55
msgid "fig = pygmt.Figure()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:63
msgid ""
"To add elements to the figure instance or object (``fig`` in this example) "
"different methods can be called on it. This example will use "
"the :meth:`pygmt.Figure.coast` method, which can be used to create a map "
"without any other methods or external data. The :meth:`pygmt.Figure.coast` "
"method plots the coastlines, borders, and bodies of water using a database "
"that is included in GMT."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:69
msgid ""
"First, a region for the figure must be selected. This example will plot some "
"of the coast of Maine in the northeastern US. A Python list can be passed to "
"the ``region`` parameter with the minimum and maximum X-values (longitude) "
"and the minimum and maximum Y-values (latitude). For this example, the "
"minimum (left bottom) coordinates are (69° W, 43.75° N) and the maximum "
"(right top) coordinates are (68° W, 44.75° N). Negative values can be passed "
"for longitudes in the western hemisphere or latitudes in the southern "
"hemisphere."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:77
msgid ""
"In addition to the region, an argument needs to be passed "
"to :meth:`pygmt.Figure.coast` to tell it what to plot. In this "
"example, :meth:`pygmt.Figure.coast` will be told to plot the shorelines by "
"passing the boolean value ``True`` to the ``shorelines`` parameter. The "
"``shorelines`` parameter has other options for finer control, but setting it "
"to ``True`` uses the default values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:85
msgid "fig.coast(region=[-69, -68, 43.75, 44.75], shorelines=True)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:93
msgid "To see the figure, call :meth:`pygmt.Figure.show`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:97
msgid "fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:107
msgid "Color the land and water"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:109
msgid ""
"This figure plots all of the coastlines in the given region, but it does not "
"indicate where the land and water are. Color values can be passed to "
"``land`` and ``water`` to set the colors on the map."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:113
msgid ""
"When plotting colors in PyGMT, there are multiple :gmt-docs:`color codes "
"<gmtcolors.html>`, that can be used. This includes standard GMT color names "
"(like ``\"skyblue\"``), R/G/B levels (like ``\"0/0/255\"``), a hex value "
"(like ``\"#333333\"``), or a gray level (like ``\"gray50\"``). For this "
"example, GMT color names are used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:121
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    region=[-69, -68, 43.75, 44.75],\n"
"    shorelines=True,\n"
"    land=\"lightgreen\",\n"
"    water=\"lightblue\",\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:138
msgid "Set the projection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:140
msgid ""
"This figure now has its colors set. By default the projection and size of "
"the map is set to ``\"Q15c+du\"``. Here, ``\"Q\"`` defines a cylindrical "
"equidistant map projection, ``\"15c+du\"`` means setting the maximum (upper) "
"map dimension to 15 cm. However, both of these values can be customized "
"according to the requirements using the ``projection`` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:146
msgid ""
"The appropriate projection varies for the type of map. The available "
"projections are explained in the :doc:`projection </projections/index>` "
"gallery. For this example, the Mercator projection is set using ``\"M\"``. "
"The width of the map will be 10 centimeters, as set by ``\"10c\"``. The map "
"size can also be set in inches using ``\"i\"`` (e.g. a 5-inch wide Mercator "
"projection would use ``\"M5i\"``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:154
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    region=[-69, -68, 43.75, 44.75],\n"
"    shorelines=True,\n"
"    land=\"lightgreen\",\n"
"    water=\"lightblue\",\n"
"    projection=\"M10c\",\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:172
msgid "Add a frame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:174
msgid ""
"While the map's colors, projection, and size have been set, the region that "
"is being displayed is not apparent. A frame can be added to annotate the "
"longitude and latitude of the region."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:178
msgid ""
"The ``frame`` parameter is used to add a frame to the map. For now, it will "
"be set to ``True`` to annotate the axes automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:183
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    region=[-69, -68, 43.75, 44.75],\n"
"    shorelines=True,\n"
"    land=\"lightgreen\",\n"
"    water=\"lightblue\",\n"
"    projection=\"M10c\",\n"
"    frame=True,\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:202
msgid "Add a title"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:204
msgid ""
"The ``frame`` parameter can also be used to add a title to the map. "
"Here, :class:`pygmt.params.Frame` sets the map title."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:209
msgid ""
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"fig.coast(\n"
"    region=[-69, -68, 43.75, 44.75],\n"
"    shorelines=True,\n"
"    land=\"lightgreen\",\n"
"    water=\"lightblue\",\n"
"    projection=\"M10c\",\n"
"    frame=Frame(axis=True, title=\"Maine\"),\n"
")\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:228
msgid "Additional exercises"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:230
msgid ""
"This is the end of the first tutorial. Here are some additional exercises "
"for the concepts that were discussed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:233
msgid ""
"Make a map of Germany using its ISO country code (\"DE\"). Pass the ISO code "
"as a Python string to the ``region`` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:236
msgid ""
"Change the color of the landmass to \"khaki\" and the water to \"azure\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:238
msgid ""
"Change the color of the lakes (using the ``lakes`` parameter) to \"red\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../intro/01_first_figure.rst:240
msgid ""
"Create a global map. Set the region to \"d\" to center the map at the Prime "
"Meridian or ``\"g\"`` to center the map at the International Date Line. When "
"the region is set without using a list full of integers or floating numbers, "
"the argument needs to be passed as a Python string. Create a map with a "
"width of 15 centimeters using the Mollweide (\"W\") projection."
msgstr ""

